Introduction to Programming with Python
I. Core Concepts in Programming
Software is a set of instructions designed for hardware to execute.
Programming is the process of writing these instructions to create software.
The written instructions are referred to as 'code'.
Every programming language has its own syntax, which is the unique set of rules required to write valid code, analogous to grammar in natural languages.
II. Why Choose Python?
Advantages of Python
Easy to learn and powerful, allowing developers to write programs using a minimal amount of code.
This conciseness makes the development process faster and simpler.
Key Example: Code Comparison
Java code for "Hello World" is verbose:


class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    System.out.println("Hello World");
  }
}
Python code is succinct:

print("Hello World")
Versatile Applications
Python is highly versatile and used across numerous technological domains:
Artificial Intelligence (AI)
Machine Learning (ML)
Big Data Analysis
Smart Devices / Internet of Things (IoT)
Cyber Security
Game Development
Backend Web Development
III. Python Fundamentals: Writing Your First Programme
The print Function
A simple Python programme to display the message "Hello World!" is:


print("Hello World!")
Expected output:

Hello World!
Printing Strings vs. Expressions
The use of quotes determines whether the system executes an expression or prints it as text:
To print the numerical result of an expression, do not include quotes:


print(2 + 5)  # Output: 7
To print the exact string representation of the expression, enclose it in quotes:

print("2 + 5")  # Output: 2 + 5
Common Syntax Mistakes to Avoid
Strictly adhering to Python's syntax rules is essential. Common errors include:
Spelling mistakes in commands (e.g., prnt instead of print).
Using incorrect casing (e.g., Print with an uppercase 'P').
Missing quotes around string literals (e.g., print(Hello World!)).
Omitting parentheses in the function call (e.g., print "Hello World!").
